   "title": "Shop freezers in seven European countries hit by cyber-attack",
   "url": "https://www.myjoyonline.com/shop-freezers-in-seven-european-countries-hit-by-cyber-attack/",
   "domain": "myjoyonline.com",
   "source": "MyJoyOnline（加纳）",
   "published": "Mon, 02 Nov 2026 17:16:19 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793639779.0,
   "ageDays": 0.05,
   "summary": "Dozens of supermarkets across sevenEuropean countries have been hit by a cyber-attack affecting their refrigerators and freezers. Stephan Moelans, the CEO of Sabcobel, which operates Delhaize's systems, on Monday told the Belga news agency that the incident was a cyber-attack and that the company had been \"working all night and morning to disconnect the affected units from the network\".",
   "summary_zh": "欧洲7个国家的数十家超市遭到网络攻击，其冷藏和冷冻设备受到影响。运营Delhaize系统的Sabcobel首席执行官Stephan Moelans于星期一告诉Belga通讯社，此次事件是一次网络攻击，公司正“整夜及整个上午忙碌，将受影响的设备与网络断开连接”。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Dozens of supermarkets across sevenEuropean countries have been hit by a cyber-attack affecting their refrigerators and freezers. Around 40 Delhaize supermarkets in Belgium and Luxembourg were affected, while a smaller number of shops were hit in Sweden, Ireland, the Netherlands, Estonia and Finland. Sabcobel, a Belgian company that manufactures some of the affected refrigerators, said the units' evaporators were switched to defrost mode, causing them to warm up, in what is believed to be the same method used in last month's incident that hit hundreds of French supermarkets. Millions of euros worth of food had to be thrown away in October, local media reported at the time. Stephan Moelans, the CEO of Sabcobel, which operates Delhaize's systems, on Monday told the Belga news agency that the incident was a cyber-attack and that the company had been \"working all night and morning to disconnect the affected units from the network\". He said since the French attack in early October, the company had been working to \"protect our customers fridges against this vulnerability\" but that the units needed physical modifications, not just software updates. Most supermarkets across Europe are linked to the internet, and maintenance and troubleshooting can be done remotely. \"The simplest solution is to manage the fridges over a secure internet connection. Most of our customers switched to that after the attack in France,\" Moelans added. The temperature of freezers in several shops across Europe reportedly rose to as high as 50C (122F). In the US, authorities are investigating what caused refrigeration malfunctions at a number of American military bases in August and working together with French officials to examine whether the incidents could be linked. Both Belgium and Sweden's national cyber-security agencies said they were monitoring the incident. The UK's National Cyber Security Centre said it was working with international partners and industry to \"fully understand details of an incident\". No incidents have been reported in the UK. In Flanders, northern Belgium, a Delhaize branch in Hoeilaart told news website Het Laatste Nieuws it had lost €100,000 (£83,500) worth of produce. The manager said that estimate did not even take into account further losses that would result from closing the shop. Carrefour, a French-owned supermarket, said the incident had only affected 15 of its Belgian stores, adding that measures had been taken immediately to ensure all shops remained open. A spokesperson for Colruyt Group, which operates Spar stores in Belgium under a franchise system, told Reuters news agency five shops had their fridge systems attacked. Sweden's budget furniture retailer Ikea said refrigeration systems of its store in the western Belgian city of Mons were affected, with a spokesman quoted by Reuters as saying \"we cannot rule out that additional locations may be affected\". A spokesperson for ICA confirmed the incident had affected a \"small number of ICA stores operating outside ICA's central network\". \"ICA is currently supporting the affected stores in managing the situation,\" they said. The store was closed earlier but has now reopened to customers. Seven out of the 800 Coop stores in Sweden were also affected but the \"situation is currently contained and under control\", the company told the BBC. In neighbouring Finland, refrigerator malfunctions were detected in some K-stores. The owner of the supermarket chain Kesko was quoted by Finland's Helsingin Sanomat newspaper as saying its security experts were investigating what caused the problem. The attack is also affecting shops in Ireland, the Netherlands and Estonia. In Ireland, it impacted 13 SuperValu supermarkets. A spokesman for the owner of the chain, Musgrave Group, confirmed to The Irish Times that a \"cyber incident\" affected its refrigeration systems, but added that the impact was limited to a \"specific model\" of refrigerator. A spokesperson for Danish manufacturer Danfoss told Dutch public radio channel NOS that the freezing and cooling systems of at least 17 companies in the Netherlands were attacked overnight, affecting butcher shops and small neighbourhood stores. A Selver supermarket in Estonia was also affected and there are reports of other affected companies in the country, according to ERR",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "eu"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "sec"
   ],
   "tier": 4,
   "actor": "第三方",
   "alsoUS": false,
   "cnHit": false,
   "usHit": false,
