   "_dt": 1793477160.0,
   "ageDays": 1.93,
   "summary": "An Associate Professor at the University of Ghana School of Law, Prof Kwadwo Appiagyei-Tuah, has praised the Ghana Gold Board (GoldBod) for its role in mobilising gold to support the economy and stabilise the Cedi but warned that the gains are being undermined by the country’s continued struggle against illegal mining. He said Ghana appeared to be making progress on the economic front through gold mobilisation, but the environmental damage caused by galamsey remained a major concern.",
   "summary_zh": "加纳大学法学院副教授夸德沃·阿皮亚吉伊-图阿赫教授称赞加纳黄金局（GoldBod）在动员黄金、支持经济以及稳定塞地方面的作用，但警告称，该国打击非法采矿的持续努力不足，正削弱这些成果。他说，加纳似乎正通过黄金动员在经济方面取得进展，但非法采矿造成的环境破坏仍是一大隐患。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "An Associate Professor at the University of Ghana School of Law, Prof Kwadwo Appiagyei-Tuah, has praised the Ghana Gold Board (GoldBod) for its role in mobilising gold to support the economy and stabilise the Cedi but warned that the gains are being undermined by the country’s continued struggle against illegal mining. He said Ghana appeared to be making progress on the economic front through gold mobilisation, but the environmental damage caused by galamsey remained a major concern. “We agree that GoldBod is doing well in getting the gold to support the economy and stabilise the Ghanaian cedi. It appears we are winning the financial war but are losing the environmental war,” he said. Prof Appiagyei-Tuah’s comments highlight the tension between the economic benefits of Ghana’s gold trade and the environmental consequences of illegal mining, particularly the pollution of water bodies and destruction of forest reserves. He had earlier acknowledged that some progress had been made in the fight against galamsey but questioned the evidence behind some of the figures being presented to support claims of success. “Definitely, some progress has been made, but there are some figures that have been thrown about without evidence,” he said. He added that although some available information suggested the government was making progress, other figures did not support that assessment, with data still indicating that illegal mining remained alarming. The comments were made on JoyNews’ Newsfile programme on Saturday, October 31, amid renewed public debate over the effectiveness of government interventions against illegal mining",
